{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"$d/blind_reread2.py\"; nl -ba \"$d/blind_adjudicate.py\"'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The D744 second blinded re-read of a v13s blind run (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D744]): the same first replies, read with the same rule text\n     2\tadjudicators get (blind_rules: system text, every schema description, the definitions), plus five controls built from fresh copies:\n     3\tK-midpoint and K-misread (P's Claude reply, its \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance as the midpoint 108 or as 108.5), K-overlap and K-disjoint\n     4\t(E's Claude reply with a growth range giving $36-43B, rung 3 with its reason or rung 4, text made consistent), and K-unsupported (E's\n     5\tClaude reply with a 140% growth estimate its own reasons argue against). Each control is checked by the code before it is used.\n     6\t\n     7\tUsage: python3 blind_reread2.py <blind run folder>\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport copy\n    10\timport hashlib\n    11\timport json\n    12\timport random\n    13\timport sys\n    14\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    15\t\n    16\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    17\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    19\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\n    23\t\n    24\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n    25\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n    26\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n    27\t\n    28\tJudge in two steps:\n    29\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n    30\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    31\t2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.\n    32\t\n    33\tReturn JSON:\n    34\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    35\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    36\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    37\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    38\t\n    39\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    40\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    41\t\"\"\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    45\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef _replace(text, old, new):\n    49\t    if text.count(old) != 1:\n    50\t        raise SystemExit(f'control text: \"{old[:60]}\" occurs {text.count(old)} times')\n    51\t    return text.replace(old, new)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef controls(out):\n    55\t    made = {}\n    56\t    p = json.loads((out / 'P' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    57\t    guide = next(f for f in p['figures'] if f.get('quote_number') == '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%')\n    58\t    for name, value in (('K-midpoint', 108.0), ('K-misread', 108.5)):\n    59\t        a = copy.deepcopy(p)\n    60\t        _fig(a, guide['id']).update(value=value, low=None, high=None)\n    61\t        made[name] = ('P', a, guide['id'])\n    62\t    e = json.loads((out / 'E' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    63\t    old_reason = 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line.'\n    64\t    for name, rung, reason in (\n    65\t            ('K-overlap', 3, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8). Its midpoint, '\n    66\t             'near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3 (Neutral), so I choose rung 3; the top of the range reaches rung 2.'),\n    67\t            ('K-disjoint', 4, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8): rung 4.')):\n    68\t        a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    69\t        f7, f8 = _fig(a, 'F7'), _fig(a, 'F8')\n    70\t        f7.update(value=None, low=80.0, high=115.0, basis_text=_replace(f7['basis_text'], 'gives 85%.', 'gives 80% to 115%.'))\n    71\t        f8.update(value=None, low=36.0, high=43.0)\n    72\t        a['answers'][0].update(rung=rung, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason, reason))\n    73\t        made[name] = ('E', a, 'F8')\n    74\t    a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    75\t    _fig(a, 'F7').update(value=140.0, basis_text='My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth: 140%, because AI demand is strong.',\n    76\t                         rests_on=['F2'])\n    77\t    _fig(a, 'F8').update(value=48.0)\n    78\t    a['answers'][0].update(rung=2, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason,\n    79\t                                                    'I forecast 140% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $48 billion (F8): rung 2 (Bullish).'))\n    80\t    made['K-unsupported'] = ('E', a, 'F7')\n    81\t    expected = {'K-midpoint': [], 'K-misread': ['S1'], 'K-overlap': [], 'K-disjoint': ['S6'], 'K-unsupported': []}\n    82\t    for name, (case, answer, _) in made.items():\n    83\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n    84\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n    85\t        if codes != expected[name]:\n    86\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the code gives {codes}, expected {expected[name]}; the control is not what it claims')\n    87\t    return made\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\tdef build(out):\n    91\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n    92\t    items = [{'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'], 'answer': f'{r[\"case\"]}/answers/{r[\"writer\"]}.json'} for r in rows]\n    93\t    folder = out / 'reread2-controls'\n    94\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n    95\t    for name, (case, answer, figure) in controls(out).items():\n    96\t        (folder / f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(answer, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        items.append({'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': f'reread2-controls/{name}.json', 'figure': figure})\n    98\t    seed = hashlib.sha256(b'D744 second re-read ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    99\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   100\t    random.Random(seed).shuffle(order)\n   101\t    packets = out / 'reread2-packets'\n   102\t    packets.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   103\t    key = {}\n   104\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   105\t        item = items[i]\n   106\t        it = json.loads((out / item['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   107\t        pid = f'T{n:02d}'\n   108\t        key[pid] = item\n   109\t        text = '\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(out / item['case']), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '', it['user'], '',\n   110\t                          '# The answer', '', (out / item['answer']).read_text(), ''])\n   111\t        (packets / f'{pid}.md').write_text(text)\n   112\t    (out / 'reread2-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   113\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {packets}; key in reread2-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   117\t    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\n   118\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n   119\t    build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve())\n     1\t\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n     2\t\n     3\tAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\n     4\tthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\n     5\tand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n     6\t\n     7\tRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\n     8\tanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\n     9\tclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n    10\t\n    11\tUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n    12\t       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n    52\t        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    53\t    return lines\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\tdef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    57\t    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    58\t    out = folder / out_name\n    59\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    60\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    61\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    62\t        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    63\t        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    64\t        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n    65\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    66\t                          format_material(folder / case), '',\n    67\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    68\t                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n    69\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    70\t        if target.exists():\n    71\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    72\t        target.write_text(text)\n    73\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    74\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    75\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\tREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n    79\t\n    80\tA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\n    81\talone:\n    82\t1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n    83\t   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n    84\t2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n    85\t   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\n    86\tReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n    87\t\"\"\"\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\tdef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    91\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    92\t    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    93\t    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n    94\t        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    95\t    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    96\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    97\t    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    98\t                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    99\t                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n   100\t                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n   101\t                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n   102\t    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n   103\t    if target.exists():\n   104\t        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n   105\t    target.write_text(text)\n   106\t    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n   107\t                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n   108\t    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   112\t    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n   113\t        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   114\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n   115\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   116\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread2':       # D744: the second re-read under the shared rule text\n   117\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread2-key.json', 'reread2-packets', 'reread2-adjudication')\n   118\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 3 and sys.argv[1] != 'refusal':\n   119\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n   120\t    else:\n   121\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"$d/blind_test.py\" | sed -n '\"'1,219p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The v13s blind conformance test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 with its [52] changes; D737, D738). $0 at the provider.\n     2\t\n     3\t  python3 blind_test.py build <out folder>      # the six registered requests, each in its own folder (system.txt, user.txt, schema.json)\n     4\t  python3 blind_test.py check <out folder>      # every writer's first answer through model.check_schema, from_answer and check\n     5\t  python3 blind_test.py packets <out folder>    # one reader packet per answer: the whole request text and the answer, checker hidden\n     6\t\n     7\tWriters put their first answer, unedited, at <out>/<case>/answers/<writer>.json (the writer label never reaches a reader).\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport hashlib\n    10\timport json\n    11\timport random\n    12\timport re\n    13\timport sqlite3\n    14\timport sys\n    15\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    16\t\n    17\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    18\tREPO = HERE.parents[2]\n    19\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    20\tsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n    21\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    22\timport paid_gate as pg  # noqa: E402\n    23\tfrom hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\n    24\t\n    25\tW = pg.W\n    26\tCASES = {   # registered in SMALL-DESIGN.md §5: archive, calls, member keys\n    27\t    'P': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'joint'},\n    28\t    'P-v4': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v4'},\n    29\t    'P-v8': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v8'},\n    30\t    'S': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v6', 9043)], 'arm': 'single',\n    31\t          'what': W('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2026')},\n    32\t    'O': {'draw': 'old-2', 'calls': [('v29', 8507)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027'),\n    33\t          'note': pg.O_NOTE},\n    34\t    'E': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v41', 9071)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Vera CPU', 'FY2028')},\n    35\t}\n    36\tREGISTERED_QUESTIONS = {\n    37\t    ('old-1', 'v4'): \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\",\n    38\t    ('old-1', 'v8'): 'How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?',\n    39\t    ('old-3', 'v6'): 'How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?',\n    40\t    ('old-2', 'v29'): \"How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\",\n    41\t    ('old-3', 'v41'): 'What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?',\n    42\t}\n    43\tREGISTERED_EDGES = {('old-1', 'v4'): [431.2, 392.0, 352.8, 313.6], ('old-1', 'v8'): [400.0, 350.0, 300.0, 250.0],\n    44\t                    ('old-3', 'v6'): [500.0, 600.0, 700.0, 800.0], ('old-2', 'v29'): [25.0, 50.0, 75.0, 100.0],\n    45\t                    ('old-3', 'v41'): [50.0, 40.0, 30.0, 20.0]}\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef heading(draw, call_id):\n    49\t    a = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{pg.RUN / draw / \"prod/archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    50\t    user = json.loads(a.execute('select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?', (call_id,)).fetchone()[0])['messages'][1]['content']\n    51\t    m = re.match(r'Thesis (v\\d+) \\([^)]*\\): ([^\\n]*)', user)\n    52\t    return m.group(1), m.group(2)\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\tdef edges(bands):\n    56\t    out = []\n    57\t    for lo, hi in bands:\n    58\t        for e in (lo, hi):\n    59\t            if e is not None and e not in out:\n    60\t                out.append(e)\n    61\t    return out\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\tdef build_case(name, spec):\n    65\t    draw = spec['draw']\n    66\t    for view_id, call_id in spec['calls']:                     # the registered heading, question and ladder, or refuse\n    67\t        tid, question = heading(draw, call_id)\n    68\t        if tid != view_id or question != REGISTERED_QUESTIONS[(draw, view_id)]:\n    69\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: call {call_id} heads {tid} \"{question}\", not the registered {view_id}')\n    70\t        got = edges(pg.bands(draw, view_id))\n    71\t        if got != REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]:                   # [54] in rung order, not sorted\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: {view_id} ladder edges {got}, not the registered {REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]}')\n    73\t    if spec['arm'] in ('joint', 'separate'):\n    74\t        (tv, tc), (dv, dc) = spec['calls']\n    75\t        family = pg.small_family(pg.pair_family(draw, (tv, tc), (dv, dc)), pg.PAIR_SCOPES)\n    76\t        if spec['arm'] == 'joint':\n    77\t            return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'joint', draw, spec['calls'], family, [tv, dv], 'v13s')\n    78\t        return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', f'separate-{spec[\"member\"]}', draw, spec['calls'], pg.only(family, spec['member']), [spec['member']], 'v13s')\n    79\t    view_id, call_id = spec['calls'][0]\n    80\t    family = {'members': {view_id: {'what': spec['what'], 'bands': pg.bands(draw, view_id)}}, 'relations': [], 'shared': [], 'compare_periods': []}\n    81\t    family = pg.small_family(family, notes={view_id: spec['note']} if spec.get('note') else None)\n    82\t    return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'single', draw, spec['calls'], family, [view_id], 'v13s')\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\tdef build(out):\n    86\t    if out.exists():\n    87\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} exists')\n    88\t    gate_quotes = {p['text'] for it in json.loads((HERE / 'paid-plan.json').read_text()) for p in it['passages'].values()}\n    89\t    report = []\n    90\t    for name, spec in CASES.items():\n    91\t        it = build_case(name, spec)\n    92\t        folder = out / name\n    93\t        (folder / 'answers').mkdir(parents=True)\n    94\t        (folder / 'system.txt').write_text(it['system'])\n    95\t        (folder / 'user.txt').write_text(it['user'])\n    96\t        (folder / 'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(it['schema'], indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        (folder / 'item.json').write_text(json.dumps(it, ensure_ascii=False))\n    98\t        fresh = sum(1 for p in it['passages'].values() if p['text'] not in gate_quotes)\n    99\t        report.append(f'{name:5} {it[\"arm\"]:13} members {it[\"members\"]} passages {len(it[\"passages\"])} ({fresh} quotes absent from the gate) '\n   100\t                      f'request {len(it[\"system\"]) + len(it[\"user\"]) + len(json.dumps(it[\"schema\"]))} chars')\n   101\t    (out / 'BUILD.txt').write_text('\\n'.join(report) + '\\n')\n   102\t    print('\\n'.join(report))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\tWRITERS = ('claude', 'codex')\n   106\t_FENCE = re.compile(r'^\\s*```(?:json)?\\s*\\n(.*)\\n\\s*```\\s*$', re.S)\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\tdef first_json(text):\n   110\t    \"\"\"[54] The writer's first output exactly as returned: one JSON object, optionally inside a single ```json fence; anything else\n   111\t    (a preface, an array, two objects) is not an answer.\"\"\"\n   112\t    m = _FENCE.match(text)\n   113\t    body = m.group(1) if m else text\n   114\t    data = json.loads(body)\n   115\t    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else None\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\tdef inventory(out):\n   119\t    \"\"\"[54] Every registered case has one first answer from each writer.\"\"\"\n   120\t    return [f'{name}/{w}' for name in CASES for w in WRITERS if not (out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').exists()]\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\tdef check(out):\n   124\t    missing = inventory(out)\n   125\t    if missing:\n   126\t        raise SystemExit(f'INCOMPLETE: no first answer for {\", \".join(missing)}')\n   127\t    rows = []\n   128\t    for name in CASES:\n   129\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n   130\t        for w in WRITERS:\n   131\t            raw = (out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').read_text()\n   132\t            row = {'case': name, 'writer': w, 'sha256': hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest()}\n   133\t            try:\n   134\t                data = first_json(raw)\n   135\t            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n   136\t                data = None\n   137\t            if data is None:\n   138\t                row.update(stage='not JSON', errors=[['format', 'not one JSON object as returned']])\n   139\t            else:\n   140\t                schema_errors = model.check_schema(data, it['schema'])\n   141\t                if schema_errors:\n   142\t                    row.update(stage='schema', errors=[['schema', e] for e in schema_errors])\n   143\t                else:\n   144\t                    try:\n   145\t                        calc = cs.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n   146\t                        result = cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n   147\t                        row.update(stage='checked', errors=[list(e) for e in result['errors']], recorded=result['recorded'],\n   148\t                                   published=result['published'], decision=cs.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, result))\n   149\t                    except Exception as error:                     # [54] recorded per answer, classed apart from the writer's errors\n   150\t                        row.update(stage='checker error', errors=[['checker', f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'[:300]]])\n   151\t            rows.append(row)\n   152\t    (out / 'CHECKED.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   153\t    for r in rows:\n   154\t        print(f'{r[\"case\"]:5} {r[\"writer\"]:10} {r[\"stage\"]:8} {r.get(\"decision\", \"-\"):9} ' + '; '.join(f'{c}: {d}' for c, d in r['errors'])[:300])\n   155\t\n   156\t\n   157\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n   158\t\n   159\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown) and one answer to it,\n   160\twritten in a JSON format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check accepted it. Read only what is given here.\n   161\t\n   162\tFor **every member** the answer forecasts, and for the answer as a whole, return JSON:\n   163\t\n   164\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n   165\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n   166\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n   167\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n   168\t\n   169\tDefinitions:\n   170\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n   171\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage writes.\n   172\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs.\n   173\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n   174\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n   175\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n   176\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n   177\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value\n   178\t  of the quarter.\n   179\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n   180\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n   181\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n   182\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n   183\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n   184\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n   185\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of the question's quantity\n   186\t  (a ruling).\n   187\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n   188\t\"\"\"\n   189\t\n   190\t\n   191\tdef packets(out):\n   192\t    if inventory(out):\n   193\t        raise SystemExit('INCOMPLETE: packets need every first answer')\n   194\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n   195\t    if len(rows) != len(CASES) * len(WRITERS):\n   196\t        raise SystemExit(f'INCOMPLETE: {len(rows)} checked answers, {len(CASES) * len(WRITERS)} registered')\n   197\t    rng = random.Random(hashlib.sha256((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest())\n   198\t    order = list(range(len(rows)))\n   199\t    rng.shuffle(order)\n   200\t    key = {}\n   201\t    folder = out / 'reader-packets'\n   202\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   203\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   204\t        r = rows[i]\n   205\t        it = json.loads((out / r['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   206\t        raw = (out / r['case'] / 'answers' / f'{r[\"writer\"]}.json').read_text()\n   207\t        pid = f'R{n:02d}'\n   208\t        key[pid] = {'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer']}\n   209\t        (folder / f'{pid}.md').write_text(READER_BRIEF + '\\n# The request as the writer saw it\\n\\n' + it['user'] + '\\n\\n# The answer\\n\\n' + raw + '\\n')\n   210\t    (out / 'reader-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   211\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {folder}; key in reader-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   212\t\n   213\t\n   214\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   215\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ''\n   216\t    if cmd in ('build', 'check', 'packets') and len(sys.argv) == 3:\n   217\t        {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets}[cmd](Path(sys.argv[2]))\n   218\t    else:\n   219\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md\" | sed -n '\"'162,348p'\"","aggregated_output":"   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   181\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   182\t  - \"-3%\";\n   183\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   184\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   185\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   186\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   187\t  - a computed external reference;\n   188\t  - an unresolved member;\n   189\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   190\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   191\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   192\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   193\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   194\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   195\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   196\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   197\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   198\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   199\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   200\t    a reason; neither.\n   201\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   202\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   203\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   204\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   205\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   206\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   207\t  - unit spellings;\n   208\t  - value with range;\n   209\t  - a four-input difference;\n   210\t  - money × % through product;\n   211\t  - \"first half\";\n   212\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   213\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   214\t\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   237\t  - a euro amount (S1).\n   238\t- **A3:** each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule (asserted). \"first half\" is refused by\n   239\t  S8 when summed. \"1.05 USD trillion\" fails the schema, and \"1050 USD bn\" passes.\n   240\t- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n   241\t  recovery. For each named defect, the defective answer fails its rule while its paired correct answer reaches the reader's card. The\n   242\t  old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.\n   243\t\n   244\t**[52] Readers.**\n   245\t- **What they get.** Both readers get the same complete shown passages, with the writer's identity and the checker's decision masked.\n   246\t- **What they mark, on every first reply:**\n   247\t  - whether each forecast is supported, naming the passage or assumption that puts it in its band;\n   248\t  - any of D737's five defects;\n   249\t  - broken structure.\n   250\t- **The wording review is separate.** Refusals are revealed only after that marking, for the review of whether the request states\n   251\t  the rule.\n   252\t- **An accepted reply with a named defect** (marked by both readers, or by the third reader) is a stop.\n   253\t- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n   254\t\n   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n   278\t\n   279\t**[D742] Registered before the third run (council rounds 56–56c).** The re-run stopped on one S4 refusal of a sound answer\n   280\t(`BLIND2-RESULT.md`). The cause was the request: the system text said to carry \"the guided growth forward\", while the schema allowed a\n   281\tguidance rate only as \"a stated benchmark for an estimate\" and the header required every path to go through the forecast. S4 is\n   282\tunchanged (rounds 56 and 56b showed that both a blanket and a narrowed exemption let a reset to guidance publish). The request now states\n   283\tone rule in the system text, the schema and the header: guidance informs the pace; the forecast pace is recorded as an estimate that\n   284\tnames its benchmark in `rests_on`; the quarter's dollar value is the one forecast figure. The rules for the third run:\n   285\t- **The stop lines are unchanged.**\n   286\t- **The refusal review** records two judgements apart for each refusal:\n   287\t  - sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound?\n   288\t  - stated: does the exact request (system text, schema and user text) clearly state the condition?\n   289\t  A sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run.\n   290\t- **The readers' brief** keeps Alex's ruling-1 sentence and adds: \"Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated\n   291\t  assumption, for a second dollar value of the quarter.\"\n   292\t- **Adjudication:**\n   293\t  - Where the two readers disagree, two adjudicators, one per family, read the packet. The packet now includes the system text.\n   294\t  - A point is settled when both agree.\n   295\t  - A settled defect on an accepted reply is a stop.\n   296\t  - A split is disclosed, not a stop.\n   297\t- **What each outcome sends where:**\n   298\t  - a fresh sound refusal sends the format back to council;\n   299\t  - an accepted reply confirmed to carry two values of a quarter sends the publication rule back to council;\n   300\t  - only a complete run that meets every line goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n   301\t- **Part A for the change:**\n   302\t  - five new cases: the stated-estimate benchmark passes; four forms of the saved refusal or a guidance reset are refused by S4;\n   303\t  - one new mutant: S4's walk following `rests_on` must fail the stated-estimate case;\n   304\t  - `s4_replay.py` on the saved reply.\n   305\t\n   306\t**[D743] The blinded re-read of the third run (Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; council round 57), registered before any packet is built.**\n   307\t- **The one change.** The readers' brief and the adjudication header add the schema's range rule to \"broken structure\": for a range\n   308\t  outcome, \"a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons\" is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is.\n   309\t- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies of `judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22`. Writers are not re-run, and the request is\n   310\t  unchanged.\n   311\t- **Two blinded controls** are built from a copy of E's Claude reply. Its growth estimate becomes a range, giving an outcome of $36–43B,\n   312\t  which crosses the $40B edge.\n   313\t  - C-overlap names rung 3 and gives its reason. It must not be marked.\n   314\t  - C-disjoint names rung 4 ($20–30B), a band the range does not reach. It must be marked broken structure.\n   315\t  Controls are scored on the readers' marks, not on support.\n   316\t- **Blinding.** 14 packets under a fresh shuffle (ids Q01–Q14, key in `reread-key.json` beside, never in the packets). The readers are\n   317\t  fresh: one Claude subagent and one Codex session per packet. They see only their packet, never earlier votes, the key or the expected\n   318\t  result.\n   319\t- **Scoring:**\n   320\t  - The registered stop lines apply to the 12 replies exactly as before, with adjudication where the readers disagree.\n   321\t  - The repair fails if either control is read wrongly by both readers, or by the adjudication when the two readers split on it.\n   322\t  - Any registered stop on the 12 replies, including S losing its only usable reply, sends the result back to council.\n   323\t  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\n   324\t\n   325\t**[D744] The second blinded re-read (Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; council round 58), registered before any packet is built.**\n   326\t- **The one change: shared rule text.** Readers get the same rule material as adjudicators, from one source (`blind_rules.py`): the\n   327\t  writer's system text, every schema description, and the definitions, which allow what the format rules permit. Readers judge in two\n   328\t  steps:\n   329\t  1. check quotes and calculations against the format rules;\n   330\t  2. name the evidence for the assumption that sets the band.\n   331\t- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies, by fresh readers (one Claude subagent and one Codex session per packet), under a fresh\n   332\t  shuffle T01–T17. The key, with answer paths, is in `reread2-key.json`, never in a packet.\n   333\t- **Five controls**, each checked by the code before use (`blind_reread2.py`), with these expected readings:\n   334\t\n   335\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   336\t  |---|---|---|\n   337\t  | K-midpoint | P's Claude reply, guidance given as the midpoint 108 | no \"number misread\" on that figure |\n   338\t  | K-misread | the same reply, guidance given as 108.5 | \"number misread\" on that figure |\n   339\t  | K-overlap | E's Claude reply with a growth range ($36–43B) and consistent text, rung 3 with its reason | no \"broken structure\" |\n   340\t  | K-disjoint | the same reply, rung 4 | \"broken structure\" |\n   341\t  | K-unsupported | E's Claude reply, 140% growth that its own reasons argue against | supported = no |\n   342\t- **Scoring:**\n   343\t  - A control fails if both readers read it wrongly, or, when the readers split on its mark, if the adjudication does.\n   344\t  - The registered stop lines apply to the 12 replies as before, with adjudication where readers disagree. The adjudication packet now\n   345\t    resolves control answers through the key.\n   346\t  - Any failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read, and the result goes back to council.\n   347\t  - Both S replies' support votes and reasons are reported beside the verdict.\n   348\t  - A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; wc -l \"$d\"/{P-v8,S,E}/user.txt \"$d\"/{P-v8,S,E}/answers/claude.json; rg -ni '\"'data center|growth|blackwell|q4|rung' \\\"\"'$d/P-v8/user.txt\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; wc -l \"$d\"/{P-v8,S,E}/user.txt \"$d\"/{P-v8,S,E}/answers/claude.json; rg -ni '\"'data center|growth|blackwell|q4|rung' \\\"\"'$d/P-v8/user.txt\"'","aggregated_output":"     771 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt\n     133 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt\n      59 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\n     481 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n     320 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\n     188 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n    1952 total\n2:Thesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n3:Ladder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.\n6:Scopes: NVIDIA revenue Data Center is part of NVIDIA revenue company. A sum never adds the part into the whole for the same period; a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest.\n7:Shared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\n8:Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v8 = NVIDIA | revenue | Data Center | FY2027.\n20:- e1510 [third_party_view; insidermonkey.com; original source insidermonkey.com 2026-07-31; from gnews:39deea97b690ac7b56dada0b 2026-07-31] NVIDIA has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin chip orders through 2027.\n21:    Quote: The company already has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed orders for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips through 2027,\n23:- e2452 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:835fa8a6ac898ca3973d586c 2026-08-23] NVIDIA guided to $65 billion in Q4 FY2026 revenue and reported actual revenue of $68 billion.\n24:    Quote: For Q4 FY 2026, Nvidia expected $65 billion in revenue; it ended up producing $68 billion.\n28:- e2652 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, with no Data Center compute revenue from China assumed.\n29:    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n42:    Quote: As of the end of the second quarter of fiscal year 2027, while we were able to ship uncontrolled products to China, such as gaming and workstation GPUs, we were effectively foreclosed from competing in China’s data center computing/compute market, and our effective foreclosure from the China market helped our competitors build larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\n44:- e2870 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0f6c59de72ffa6b9a2036962 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said demand for NVIDIA's chips was far greater than 70%, while memory, power, and data center capacity constrained growth to that pace.\n45:    Quote: Huang said actual demand for Nvidia's chips is far greater than 70%, but limited memory, power, and data center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now.\n47:- e2882 [party_statement; marketbeat.com; original source Colette Kress ; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in FY2027 Q3; Kress said it is expected to be NVIDIA's fastest product ramp to date.\n48:    Quote: Kress said NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to be its fastest product ramp to date and expects the platform to account for about 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter.\n51:    Quote: Its Q3 outlook calls for revenue of $108 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 89%.\n54:    Quote: severe supply chain bottlenecks—ranging from memory availability to data center power and construction—are capping the company's near-term output.\n56:- e3200 [fact; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ef1c09ba4c3333679aa5e869 2026-08-27] NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue exceeded the $91.0 billion midpoint of its outlook, which excluded China Data Center compute revenue, by $5.2 billion.\n57:    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) had also excluded China Data Center compute revenue from its $91.0 billion second-quarter outlook, yet actual revenue exceeded that midpoint by $5.2 billion.\n58:- e3280 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; from gnews:b39d8c4b1f73b622347a66e4 2026-08-28] CFO Colette Kress guided to 70% revenue growth for NVIDIA's full fiscal year, ahead of analyst estimates.\n59:    Quote: As if this weren't enough, Kress guided 70% growth for the full fiscal year, which was ahead of analyst estimates.\n60:- e3346 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:871ea3cb33e5728421ec2d5e 2026-08-28] Nvidia guided third-quarter revenue to $105.84 billion–$110.16 billion, implying 85.7%–93.2% year-over-year growth from $57.01 billion.\n61:    Quote: For the third quarter, Nvidia expects revenue between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 85.7% to 93.2% from the $57.01 billion reported in the same quarter last year.\n68:- e3890 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0295f27f6c1a83738ef035a9 2026-09-01] NVIDIA projected revenue growth of 70% in 2027, versus analysts’ forecast of 44%.\n69:    Quote: the company's projection of 70% revenue growth in 2027, smashing analysts' forecast of 44%.\n82:    Quote: In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, the growth rate moderated to 65%, while revenue reached $215.9 billion.\n84:- e4283 [party_statement; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA forecast that most FY2027 Q3 growth would come from ACIE, with hyperscale growth accelerating in Q4 and throughout FY2028 as Vera Rubin supply expands.\n85:    Quote: According to Nvidia’s forecast, most of the growth in the third quarter is expected to come from ACIE, while growth in hyperscale is expected to accelerate again in the fourth quarter and throughout fiscal 2028 as the supply of the Vera Rubin platform expands.\n92:- e4574 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:94e5676a81cad9dd9b8ed3d3 2026-09-10] Amazon and NVIDIA announced on August 26 an expanded deal for an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs, on top of AWS’s earlier plan for more than 1 million GPUs starting in 2026; the additional GPUs, including Rubin, are expected to arrive in AWS data centers in 2027 and 2028.\n93:    Quote: Amazon.com, Inc. (NASDAQ: AMZN ) and NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) announced an expanded deal on August 26 that will bring an additional 2 million Nvidia GPU processors to Amazon data centers, building on AWS's earlier plan to add more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs starting in 2026. The new processors, which include NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs, are expected to arrive in \n95:- e4575 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:94e5676a81cad9dd9b8ed3d3 2026-09-10] NVIDIA is targeting more than $1 trillion in Blackwell and Rubin revenue through 2027.\n96:    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) gains a massive buyer as the company targets more than $1 trillion in Blackwell and Rubin revenue through 2027\n100:- e4628 [party_statement; Matthew Benjamin; original source NVIDIA management (revenue guidance) and analysts (consensus estimate) ; from gnews:97a0223f09c1a04dfbc35cc1 2026-09-10] NVIDIA management expects third-quarter revenue of $108 billion and full-year revenue growth of 70%; the article gives analyst consensus full-year revenue growth of 44%.\n101:    Quote: Nvidia's management sees revenue rising to $108 billion in the third quarter, and expects full-year revenue to soar 70% over last year, which is much higher than the 44% growth consensus projection from analysts.\n115:- e4899 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:13952dff579190ce77353acd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA guided to approximately $108 billion in total revenue, plus or minus 2%, for FY2027 Q3, implying 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\n116:    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\n142:- e4923 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Madison Rezaei ; from gnews:5c6f1264f400504437456ed5 2026-09-14] Bernstein analyst Madison Rezaei expects demand for rural data centers built for AI training to weaken if training activity slows.\n160:    Quote: Again. Just three weeks ago, Nvidia reported its fiscal second-quarter financial results. The company posted adjusted EPS of $2.22 on revenue of $96.22 billion. That beat Wall Street by more than $4.0 billion and was good for year-over-year growth of 106%.\n162:- e4963 [party_statement; Stephen Guilfoyle; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2fc71ee9730f9420d6d130bd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA projected current-quarter revenue of $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, versus consensus of $104.6 billion; the projection implied roughly 90% year-over-year growth.\n163:    Quote: For the current quarter, CEO Jensen Huang’s firm projected revenue of $108.0 billion (+/-2%) vs. consensus for $104.6 billion. That would be good for growth of roughly 90% and Nvidia Is known for beating its guidance.\n186:- e4990 [third_party_view; Zacks.com analyst; original source Zacks.com analyst 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks consensus estimates put NVIDIA fiscal 2027 revenue growth at 87.4% year over year and EPS growth at 93.5%.\n213:- e5008 [party_statement; Zacks analyst; original source Broadcom 2026-09-02; from gnews:955c64e5a5907651550e94b8 2026-09-15] Broadcom guided fiscal Q4 2026 AI semiconductor revenue to $21.7 billion, up 236% year over year.\n233:- e5025 [fact; fool.com, author Leo Sun; original source AMD ; from gnews:596e206a8275549b0287e1e4 2026-09-15] AMD Data Center revenue, including Instinct GPUs and Epyc CPUs, rose 107% year over year and was 58% of total revenue in its latest quarter.\n234:    Quote: In AMD's latest quarter, its data center revenue -- which includes both its Instinct GPUs and Epyc CPUs -- rose 107% but only accounted for 58% of its top line.\n235:- e5032 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management targeted approximately $108 billion in total revenue for the third quarter; the article also reported latest-quarter Data Center revenue of $89 billion, about 92.5% of quarterly sales.\n244:- e5044 [third_party_view; simplywall.st; original source The same storyline ; from gnews:6a740db073e65b758284a46c 2026-09-15] The valuation framework projects NVIDIA annual revenue above $302.97 billion and net income of $192.88 billion, attributing them to its data center, gaming, and automotive businesses.\n245:    Quote: In this framework, more than $302.97 billion in annual revenue and $192.88 billion in net income are tied back to an ecosystem that spans data center infrastructure, gaming graphics, and automotive compute rather than a single product cycle.\n246:- e5047 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter total revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117%.\n247:    Quote: Nvidia generated $96.2 billion of revenue in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, up 106% year-over-year (YoY), while data center revenue reached $89 billion, up 117%.\n269:- e5061 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management expected ACIE to drive most of Data Center's sequential revenue growth in Q3, with hyperscaler demand potentially picking up more strongly in Q4 and FY2028.\n270:    Quote: Management expects ACIE to drive most of the sequential revenue growth for Data Center in the third quarter, while hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter and in fiscal 2028.\n272:- e5062 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said the ongoing Vera Rubin system ramp was expected to support NVIDIA revenue growth.\n273:    Quote: The ongoing ramp of Nvidia's next-generation computing system, Vera Rubin, is also expected to support revenue growth.\n274:- e5065 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA estimated revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure would rise from about $25 billion with Blackwell to $40 billion with Vera Rubin, partly due to additional CPUs, networking products, and other system components.\n275:    Quote: Nvidia estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure will increase from about $25 billion with Blackwell systems to $40 billion with Vera Rubin systems, partly because the newer platform includes more CPUs, networking products, and other system components.\n282:- e5068 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said anticipated Q4 hyperscaler revenue reacceleration could mean less Q3 revenue upside from hyperscalers than from ACIE or other drivers.\n283:    Quote: Additionally, since hyperscaler revenue is expected to reaccelerate in the fourth quarter, it may provide less upside to third-quarter revenue results than ACIE or other growth drivers.\n287:- e5071 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 revenue guidance assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China.\n288:    Quote: Nvidia's third-quarter revenue guidance also assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n313:    Quote: Nvidia has also started shipping systems based on its next-generation Vera Rubin architecture, providing another potential source of growth.\n321:- e5099 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:906d80c706aa948ee141e852 2026-09-16] Jensen Huang said roughly $400 billion of venture financing flowed into AI companies over six months, creating demand for compute and data centers.\n322:    Quote: Huang said roughly $400 billion in venture financing flowed into AI companies over six months, creating jobs and massive demand for compute, which is subsequently driving demand for data centers.\n334:- e5105 [fact; fool.com (author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:147e79a4a1f10279da449944 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $96.2 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 106% year over year, with about 92% from Data Center.\n335:    Quote: Companywide revenue totaled $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, and about 92% of it came from the data center business.\n343:    Quote: Management's forecast for the fiscal third quarter, now underway, is $108.0 billion of revenue -- and growth like that is ultimately a claim about what customers keep spending.\n360:- e5120 [third_party_view; Zacks; original source IDC ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] IDC forecast semiconductor revenue growth of 52.8% in calendar 2026, driven primarily by AI infrastructure investment.\n361:    Quote: IDC also expects exceptional growth in 2026, with semiconductor revenue rising 52.8%, driven overwhelmingly by AI infrastructure investment.\n364:- e5123 [third_party_view; Zacks; original source Technavio ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] Technavio estimates military and aerospace semiconductor-market growth at a 6% CAGR through 2029.\n366:- e5124 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported FY2027 second-quarter revenue growth of 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n367:    Quote: NVIDIA's second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, while Data Center revenue jumped 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n375:- e5129 [third_party_view; Zacks; original source Analysts ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] Analysts forecast NVIDIA revenue growth of 88% and earnings growth of 93.5% in FY2027, followed by revenue growth of 65.5% and earnings growth of 66% in FY2028.\n379:- e5136 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] Vera Rubin shipments started in August 2026, and NVIDIA management expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n380:    Quote: Vera Rubin shipments started in August 2026, and management expects that platform to be about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n405:    Quote: For the remainder of fiscal 2027, Wall Street analysts expect 90% growth in Q3\n406:- e5162 [third_party_view; fool.com author; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:ea4633ed3e4ad9d71383673f 2026-09-17] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 65% year over year in FY2027 Q4.\n407:    Quote: and 65% growth in Q4.\n408:- e5163 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Emerald AI, Google, and NVIDIA 2026-09-17; from gnews:4bf9544d82b0d4574011d0a2 2026-09-17] Emerald AI formed a coalition with Google and NVIDIA to use Emerald AI’s technology to secure grid capacity for additional data centers.\n409:    Quote: Grid software unicorn Emerald AI has formed a coalition with Google and Nvidia that will use the startup’s technology to secure space on the grid for more data centers.\n410:- e5164 [party_statement; TechCrunch, reporting AEMA’s claim; original source AI Energy Management Alliance ; from gnews:4bf9544d82b0d4574011d0a2 2026-09-17] The AI Energy Management Alliance said demand response could enable an additional 100 GW of data centers to connect to the grid.\n411:    Quote: Pausing noncritical tasks and shifting some compute loads, AEMA says, could allow an additional 100 gigawatts of data centers to connect to the grid.\n416:- e5172 [third_party_view; Hayden Capital, as quoted by Insider Monkey; original source Hayden Capital ; from gnews:81badd809629cf7b5ba30af9 2026-09-17] Hayden Capital said supply growth was already beginning, citing recent announcements from SK Hynix, Samsung, and CXMT.\n424:- e5193 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source ASUS 2026-09-17; from gnews:4425f01994c0a126351f2bad 2026-09-17] ASUS began shipping its ExpertCenter Pro ET900N G3 deskside AI supercomputer, powered by NVIDIA’s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Desktop Superchip.\n426:- e5194 [fact; ASUS and Poesis, in a Business Wire release republished by finance.yahoo.com; original source ASUS and Poesis 2026-09-17; from gnews:4425f01994c0a126351f2bad 2026-09-17] ASUS and Poesis reported completing a week-long experiment deploying autonomous AI agents to trade in financial markets on an ASUS system powered by NVIDIA’s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Desktop Superchip.\n458:- e5223 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided next-quarter total revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, citing continued strength in its AI Data Center business as the primary growth driver.\n459:    Quote: First, it guided total revenue for the next quarter to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said growth would be driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business.\n467:- e5234 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote grid-responsive AI data centers in exchange for faster grid connections.\n468:    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n470:- e5235 [third_party_view; qz.com, reporting Sivaram's remarks according to Engadget; original source Varun Sivaram, Emerald AI CEO ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said new data centers can wait ten years or longer for grid access, while average grid utilization is around 50%.\n471:    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n472:- e5236 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing Fortune; original source Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] A poll cited by Fortune found roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community, chiefly citing local power strain and higher utility bills.\n473:    Quote: Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community , citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills as their leading concerns.\n474:- e5237 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing Data Center Watch data reported by Fortune; original source Data Center Watch ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year.\n475:    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n482:    Quote: Later this year in Virginia, NVIDIA, Digital Realty and Emerald AI will turn on the world's first power-flexible AI factory at nearly 100 megawatts, designed to prove that a data center can be a precise, controllable load, instead of drawing constant, unyielding power 24/7 and putting unprecedented strain on a rigid grid.\n484:    Quote: London-based AI data center company Nscale has filed publicly for an initial public offering (IPO) in New York to acquire the infrastructure needed to meet the global demand for artificial intelligence compute power.\n502:- e5262 [party_statement; benzinga.com, reporting Huang's CNBC interview; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:7218ec5f13c26358ef736b14 2026-09-18] Huang expects AI testing to become a third major infrastructure category alongside training and inference, requiring additional data centers and creating another source of compute demand.\n503:    Quote: In fact, Huang expects testing to become a third major category of AI infrastructure alongside training and inference. He said large testing environments will require additional data centers, creating another source of compute demand.\n506:- e5265 [fact; BGR.com, reporting NVIDIA’s announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-18; from gnews:109fc9fd6937796c01c45a8c 2026-09-18] NVIDIA announced a partnership with Google and Emerald AI to form the AI Energy Management Alliance, which is intended to develop and manage flexible AI data centers.\n509:- e5266 [third_party_view; Varun Sivaram, quoted by Fortune and retold by BGR.com; original source Varun Sivaram ; from gnews:109fc9fd6937796c01c45a8c 2026-09-18] Emerald AI founder Varun Sivaram estimated that flexible data centers could free up 100 GW of power.\n510:    Quote: By Sivaram's calculations, flexible data centers could free up 100 GW of power, which would be reflected not only in electricity use but also bills.\n527:- e5280 [rumour; Channel Insider; original source Channel Insider ; from gnews:7d83454ace7462ccdd8aaeb9 2026-09-18] DeepSeek reportedly plans to deploy 160,000 Huawei AI chips at a data center in Inner Mongolia.\n528:    Quote: DeepSeek reportedly plans to deploy 160,000 Huawei AI chips at a data center in Inner Mongolia , a project that could further expand Huawei’s role in China’s AI infrastructure market.\n532:- e5286 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom's FY2026 guidance implied 186% growth in AI revenue for FY2026.\n533:    Quote: fiscal year 2026 guidance implied 186% annual AI revenue growth\n540:- e5289 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Intel Corporation ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] Intel reported second-quarter data center and server revenue of $6.26 billion, up 59% year over year.\n541:    Quote: The firm's second quarter results saw its data center and server business's revenue sit at $6.26 billion to beat analyst estimates of $5.37 billion. The growth for the business was 59% which suprassed overall revenue growth of 25%.\n549:- e5294 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] NVIDIA's Q3 guidance implied 117% year-over-year total revenue growth.\n550:    Quote: its Q3 guidance implied a 117% annual revenue growth.\n551:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e4523 (2026-08-26); both are in force: NVIDIA management guided to total revenue growth of about 90% year over year in FY2027 Q3.\n568:- e5302 [fact; tedmag.com; original source Emerald AI, Google and NVIDIA 2026-09-18; from gnews:8c54ac0fb564af052a3f1062 2026-09-18] Emerald AI, Google and NVIDIA announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to advance data centers that adjust electricity use to grid conditions.\n569:    Quote: Today, Emerald AI , Google and NVIDIA announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance (AEMA) , a first-of-its-kind coalition advancing data centers that can dynamically manage their electricity use in response to grid conditions.\n579:- e109 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in fiscal Q1 2027, equal to 92% of total sales.\n580:    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales.\n582:- e436 [fact; fool.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported $193.7 billion of Data Center revenue in FY2026.\n583:    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\n586:- e1135 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA's Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n587:    Quote: Even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n588:- e2295 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar cites NVIDIA Data Center revenue of $3 billion in FY2020 and $194 billion in FY2026, and estimates $361 billion in FY2027, up 86% year over year.\n589:    Quote: Nvidia’s DC business has achieved exponential growth already, rising from $3 billion in fiscal 2020 to $194 billion in fiscal 2026, and we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027, representing 86% annual growth.\n592:- e2650 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\n593:    Quote: Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago\n595:- e2681 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its Q3 FY2027 outlook assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n596:    Quote: NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n598:- e2691 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said customer forecasts indicate demand for its growth would double next year, while its supply-constrained revenue outlook is approximately 70% growth in FY2028.\n599:    Quote: Customers forecasts point to our growth, doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply constrained.\n604:- e2721 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\n605:    Quote: We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\n607:- e2723 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\n608:    Quote: Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\n610:- e3399 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that Data Center revenue rose from 87.9% of NVIDIA total revenue in fiscal Q2 2026 to 92.5% in fiscal Q2 2027, increasing each quarter in between.\n611:    Quote: A year ago, in the second quarter of fiscal 2026, data center revenue was 87.9% of Nvidia's total. The share rose to 89.8% the next quarter, then 91.5%, then 92.2%, then 92.5% in the quarter just reported.\n613:- e3401 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion in fiscal Q3 2027 if NVIDIA total revenue reaches its $108.0 billion guidance midpoint and Data Center remains 92.5% of the mix.\n614:    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n615:- e3406 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA said hyperscale-business growth should reaccelerate in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n616:    Quote: And the company said growth in its hyperscale business (sales to the giant cloud computing providers and the biggest consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n620:- e4202 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 2026-09-04] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA could reach $40 billion in revenue per gigawatt of computing capacity installed with Vera Rubin, compared with about $25 billion with Blackwell and $18 billion with Hopper.\n621:    Quote: Nvidia used to collect about $18 billion in revenue for every gigawatt of computing capacity it helped install with Hopper. With Blackwell, that figure rose to $25 billion. Kress says that with Vera Rubin, the company can reach $40 billion per gigawatt.\n626:    Quote: Approximately 92% of Nvidia’s revenue comes from data center infrastructure, including chips and connectivity.\n628:- e4519 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:fd039f68d65be67fc1005bcc 2026-09-09] NVIDIA's ACIE business—sovereign, NeoCloud and enterprise Data Center revenue—grew 138% year over year to $40 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.\n629:    Quote: ACIE, the sovereign, NeoCloud and enterprise side of the data center business, grew 138% year over year to $40 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.\n631:- e4803 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ff9cd0726de49630ffec230 2026-09-12] In the second quarter, NVIDIA's ACIE customers grew 25% sequentially versus 13% growth from hyperscale cloud providers, and ACIE represented roughly half of Data Center revenue.\n632:    Quote: During the second quarter, the ACIE segment delivered 25% sequential growth (versus 13% from the hyperscale cloud providers) and now represents roughly half of data center revenue.\n633:- e4897 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:13952dff579190ce77353acd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $89 billion in FY2027 Q2, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n634:    Quote: Data Center revenues came in at $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% from the previous quarter.\n636:- e4907 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7708a5ba7485f4ee381ccc41 2026-09-14] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $83.7 billion out of $96.22 billion in total revenue for the second quarter.\n637:    Quote: the data center business accounting for $83.7 billion out of the $96.22 billion of the total revenue.\n638:- e4926 [fact; fxleaders.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4dca50e22bd9f158d2a04186 2026-09-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 Data Center revenue of $89 billion.\n639:    Quote: Data center revenue rose to $89 billion\n641:- e4927 [fact; fxleaders.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4dca50e22bd9f158d2a04186 2026-09-14] NVIDIA’s fiscal Q2 Data Center revenue was supported by $49 billion from hyperscaler activity.\n671:- e4953 [fact; investingnews.com; original source Google ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Google is providing financial guarantees to help Hut 8 raise debt for separate Anthropic data centers designed for TPUs.\n672:    Quote: Google is actively providing financial guarantees to help Hut 8 raise debt for separate Anthropic data centers designed specifically to house TPUs.\n674:- e4985 [fact; Zacks.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89.02 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, more than 92% of total sales.\n675:    Quote: During the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the data center business generated $89.02 billion in revenues, accounting for more than 92% of total sales.\n677:- e4986 [fact; Zacks.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose 117% year over year and 18% sequentially in fiscal Q2 2027.\n680:- e4993 [fact; TradingView; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3da38213d30cedb4fc122748 2026-09-14] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion last quarter, up 117% year over year.\n681:    Quote: Even as Data Center revenue surged 117% year-over-year to $89 billion last quarter, Nvidia is now pushing to broaden who consumes its compute, not just who buys its GPUs.\n683:- e4995 [party_statement; TradingView; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3da38213d30cedb4fc122748 2026-09-14] NVIDIA expects non-hyperscaler businesses to account for roughly half of its Data Center business.\n684:    Quote: Nvidia expects non-hyperscaler businesses — including sovereign AI, regional neoclouds, enterprises, edge computing and air-gapped data centers — to represent roughly half of its Data Center business.\n686:- e5003 [fact; Zacks analyst; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:955c64e5a5907651550e94b8 2026-09-15] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n687:    Quote: The Data Center business remained the key growth driver, generating $89 billion in revenues, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n689:- e5024 [fact; fool.com, author Leo Sun; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:596e206a8275549b0287e1e4 2026-09-15] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose 117% year over year and represented 92% of total revenue in its latest quarter.\n690:    Quote: In Nvidia's latest quarter, its data center revenue surged 117% year over year and accounted 92% of its top line.\n692:- e5045 [third_party_view; simplywall.st; original source The NVIDIA narrative ; from gnews:6a740db073e65b758284a46c 2026-09-15] The article says NVIDIA Data Center revenue accounts for more than 70% of total revenue and is growing rapidly.\n693:    Quote: Data Center Revenue: Now more than 70% of total revenue and growing rapidly.\n694:- e5060 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said ACIE accounted for about 45% of NVIDIA's Data Center revenue.\n695:    Quote: ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue.\n696:- e5064 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan estimated Vera Rubin systems would contribute about 20% of NVIDIA Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\n697:    Quote: Vera Rubin systems are expected to contribute about one-fifth of Nvidia's Data Center revenue in the third quarter.\n701:- e5079 [fact; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.02 billion, up 117% year over year.\n702:    Quote: Data Center alone at $89.02 billion, up 117%\n704:- e5109 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n705:    Quote: NVIDIA's Data Center business remains the primary growth driver, generating $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% from a year ago and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\n707:- e5116 [fact; GuruFocus; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9a8cf969d7dedc50100397a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in its latest quarter, approximately 92.5% of total quarterly sales.\n724:- e5185 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:509f491c104308dfdd480587 2026-09-17] NVIDIA Data Center revenue reached $89 billion in its latest reported quarter.\n727:- e5199 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:824eb11f9b37d2a3489d6e59 2026-09-18] NVIDIA reported second-quarter total revenue of $96 billion, up 106% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year.\n728:    Quote: The company recently reported terrific second-quarter results, with revenue up 106% year over year to $96 billion, and revenue from data centers up 117% to $89 billion.\n730:- e5218 [fact; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3742f4ebc0994af8eb404431 2026-09-18] NVIDIA Data Center revenue more than doubled to $89 billion in the second quarter.\n733:- e5221 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated about $89 billion in revenue in the quarter ended July 26, 2026.\n734:    Quote: About $89 billion of that came from its data center segment.\n736:- e5227 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-09-18; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] The author used NVIDIA's roughly 70% FY2028 total-revenue growth outlook and its latest-quarter Data Center revenue of $89 billion as a proxy for AI revenue, reasoning that the segment is overwhelmingly driven by AI computing demand.\n737:    Quote: Nvidia expects total revenue to grow about 70% in fiscal 2028, starting from an already enormous base of nearly $100 billion per quarter. The company does not report a stand-alone \"AI revenue\" figure, but data center revenue ($89 billion in the latest quarter) is a reasonable proxy, since that business is overwhelmingly driven by AI computing demand.\n739:- e5263 [party_statement; benzinga.com, reporting Huang's CNBC interview; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:7218ec5f13c26358ef736b14 2026-09-18] Huang said NVIDIA Grace Blackwell systems rent for about $16 per GPU hour now, versus roughly $5 under some contracts signed a year earlier.\n740:    Quote: Huang also pointed to rising rental prices as evidence of tight demand. He said NVIDIA Grace Blackwell systems can now rent for about $16 per GPU hour, compared with roughly $5 under some contracts signed a year ago.\n745:- e5293 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d7c05e7b0777edd1a79bc806 2026-09-18] NVIDIA reported Q2 Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year.\n746:    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s data center revenue doubled to $89 billion in Q2 for a 117% bump\n755:- e5309 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA reported $164 billion of Data Center segment revenue in the first six months of FY2027.\n756:    Quote: The company has generated $164 billion in revenue from its data center segment in the first six months of fiscal 2027.\n757:- e5310 [third_party_view; Harsh Chauhan, fool.com; original source Harsh Chauhan 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] The author calculated that NVIDIA’s first-six-month FY2027 Data Center revenue implies a full-year run rate of just under $330 billion.\n759:- e5312 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $89 billion in FY2027 Q2, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n760:    Quote: The Data Center business remained the key growth driver, with revenues rising 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion.\n764:- v16 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No market consensus is supplied. The deadline reference is passage by December 31, 2026 (rung 1); the alternative is that the Senate has not passed RASA by then (rung 2). RASA had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23, and the August 19 report says it had still not passed.\n765:- v22 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan’s Ministry of Economic Affairs adopt performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The reference is rung 2: no formal adoption by the deadline is indicated in the available plan or timeline. The consultations and reported consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry set no timeline; that evidence does not make formal adoption by January 31, 2027 the mor\n766:- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan adopt a criminal ban on all AI chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the supplied evidence says Taiwan is considering a ban, not that it has adopted one. That consideration, in the context of an investigation into suspected smuggling, raises the prospect of action, but the evidence gives no adopted measure, legislative stage, or timetable. Th\n767:- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms’ access to advanced U.S. chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Commerce completion deadline is supplied, and the stated status is a formal investigation, not a scheduled conclusion. The evidence makes non-conclusion by December 31, 2026 more likely: Commerce is still examining access through computing capacit\n768:- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The market reference for the four companies is roughly $720–745 billion, which sits in rung 4. The evidence implies about $760 billion: the direct four-hyperscaler estimate is $760 billion, while the near-$795–800 billion estimates are for five hyperscalers and should not be assigned wholly to these\n769:- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-18; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The four-company market reference is about $930–$980 billion (rung 3): BofA's $1.08 trillion 2027 estimate covers five hyperscalers, and the carried estimate for Oracle is roughly $100–$150 billion. The higher NVIDIA forecast of $1.3 trillion for five implies roughly $1.15–$1.20 trillion for these f\n770:- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-09-01; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the status quo of no Chinese authorization by the deadline, consistent with NVIDIA's zero-China-revenue Q3 outlook, though that guidance is not itself evidence that China will not authorize purchases. The evidence still makes limited authorization more likely than none: e244\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
