{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; rg -n -i '730|700|745|575|individual|majority|Microsoft.*[0-9]|80%|90%|spending' \\\"\"'$d/S/user.txt\" | cut -c1-425; nl -ba \"$d/P-v8/answers/codex.json\" | sed -n '\"'74,156p'\"","aggregated_output":"3:Ladder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n12:- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n13:    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n17:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n18:    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n27:- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n28:    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n30:    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n32:- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n33:    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n35:- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n36:    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n47:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n48:    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n52:- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n53:    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n57:- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n58:    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n60:    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n69:- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n73:- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n74:    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n76:    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n84:    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n86:- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n87:    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n104:- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n105:    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n113:- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n114:    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n123:- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n    74\t      \"inputs\": [\"F5\", \"F8\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n    75\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    76\t    },\n    77\t    {\n    78\t      \"id\": \"F10\",\n    79\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n    80\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 15, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    81\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n    82\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n    83\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate 15% sequential growth from my Q3 forecast. Management expects hyperscaler growth to pick up as Rubin supply builds, and CoreWeave already has a multi-rack Rubin deployment online. I expect a larger dollar gain on the higher Q3 base, with memory and long delivery lead times limiting the pace.\",\n    84\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F8\", \"F9\", \"e3406\", \"e5118\", \"e5067\", \"e5205\"],\n    85\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    86\t    },\n    87\t    {\n    88\t      \"id\": \"F11\",\n    89\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n    90\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 128.36, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    91\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n    92\t      \"inputs\": [\"F9\", \"F10\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n    93\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    94\t    },\n    95\t    {\n    96\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n    97\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"share of company revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n    98\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 93, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    99\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   100\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n   101\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate a modest rise from the reported Q2 mix because NVIDIA identifies Data Center as the main driver of Q3 growth. The estimate allows Edge Computing revenue to grow as well.\",\n   102\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F3\", \"F5\", \"e3399\", \"e5059\", \"e5223\"],\n   103\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   104\t    },\n   105\t    {\n   106\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n   107\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"share of company revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n   108\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 93.5, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n   109\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   110\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n   111\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate another modest mix gain as hyperscaler sales pick up with Rubin availability. This leaves room for sequential growth in the rest of the company.\",\n   112\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F12\", \"e3399\", \"e3406\", \"e5059\", \"e5118\"],\n   113\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   114\t    },\n   115\t    {\n   116\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n   117\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n   118\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 103.8, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   119\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   120\t      \"inputs\": [\"F9\", \"F12\"], \"operation\": \"share\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n   121\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   122\t    },\n   123\t    {\n   124\t      \"id\": \"F15\",\n   125\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n   126\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 120.01, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   127\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   128\t      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F13\"], \"operation\": \"share\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n   129\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   130\t    },\n   131\t    {\n   132\t      \"id\": \"F16\",\n   133\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"},\n   134\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 223.81, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   135\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   136\t      \"inputs\": [\"F14\", \"F15\"], \"operation\": \"sum\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n   137\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   138\t    },\n   139\t    {\n   140\t      \"id\": \"F17\",\n   141\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n   142\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 388.08, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   143\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null,\n   144\t      \"inputs\": [\"F4\", \"F16\"], \"operation\": \"sum\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n   145\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v8\"], \"reference_for\": []\n   146\t    }\n   147\t  ],\n   148\t  \"answers\": [\n   149\t    {\n   150\t      \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n   151\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   152\t      \"rung\": 2,\n   153\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   154\t      \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $388 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also falls in rung 2. The reported first half contributes $164.27 billion. I forecast about $104 billion in Q3 and $120 billion in Q4. NVIDIA's Q3 company guidance anchors the pace. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a modest beat, and the live CoreWeave deployment and management's expectation of faster hyperscaler growth support a further Q4 step-up. Memory constraints and long delivery lead times limit how much of that demand can ship by year-end, keeping the forecast below $400 billion. The calendar 2026 spending theme supports demand this fiscal year. The higher calendar 2027 spending theme offers some late-year upside, with most of the spending window falling after NVIDIA's fiscal close. Possible H200 authorization adds limited Q4 upside because authorization may come late and shipment timing is uncertain. The other export-policy themes leave near-term shipment assumptions unchanged.\",\n   155\t      \"why\": null,\n   156\t      \"cites\": [\"e2295\", \"e109\", \"e5079\", \"e5078\", \"e2652\", \"e3200\", \"e5056\", \"e2693\", \"e5061\", \"e5118\", \"e3406\", \"e3399\", \"e5059\", \"e5223\", \"e5067\", \"e5205\"],\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"$d/RESULT.md\" | sed -n '\"'73,126p'; rg -n 'v13s|judging-v13s.txt|paid-plan|def item|def pack' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\"; nl -ba \"$d/BLIND3-RESULT.md\" | sed -n '\"'99,164p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '440,470p'; rg -n '\"'^## D721'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '194,236p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"$d/RESULT.md\" | sed -n '\"'73,126p'; rg -n 'v13s|judging-v13s.txt|paid-plan|def item|def pack' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\"; nl -ba \"$d/BLIND3-RESULT.md\" | sed -n '\"'99,164p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '440,470p'; rg -n '\"'^## D721'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '194,236p'\"","aggregated_output":"    73\t## The interim for a first release\n    74\t\n    75\tPublish questions, evidence and events, and show number questions as \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the forecast text and its\n    76\thistory withheld together (`bundle.py`).\n    77\t\n    78\t## The one test (registered with the design)\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The $0 gate.** Hand-reconstruct, in the new format:\n    81\t  - old-2 8729/8737, 8734 and 8595, and old-3 9166;\n    82\t  - the four gold controls;\n    83\t  - three planted errors (an amount absent from its quote, a wrong sum, an internal forecast labelled consensus).\n    84\t\n    85\t  The format must expose every defect, represent every control, and fail every plant. Any miss stops the line.\n    86\t- **The paid gate** (Alex's ceiling; 36 calls, about $0.2–0.4 estimated):\n    87\t  - **The arms:** the 09-20 total and Data Center pair in each draw, three samples each, judged jointly and separately under the same\n    88\t    format and checks. Plus three samples each on the O, S and E requests. Every output is read against its source with the arm\n    89\t    hidden.\n    90\t  - **Pass:** the joint arm publishes both answers usable and source-supported in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot,\n    91\t    and at least 3 more usable pairs than the separate arm. Zero published hard contradictions, internal-as-market references, or\n    92\t    unresolved overlap, scope or unsized adjustments that decide the band.\n    93\t  - **Stop:** any such published defect, either coverage line missed, or an incomplete comparison.\n35:SYSTEM_S = (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text()                      # the small format (D737): one sentence changed\n36:CHECKERS = {None: cc, 'v13': cc, 'v13s': cs}\n38:PINNED_PLAN_SHA256 = (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').read_text().strip() if (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').exists() else None\n103:    return cs.from_answer(data, family) if fmt == 'v13s' else strip(data)\n126:def item(unit, arm, draw, calls, family, shown, fmt='v13'):\n128:    if fmt == 'v13s':\n129:        user, evidence_ids, theme_ids = rv.render(archive, calls, family, shown=shown, fmt='v13s')\n130:        return {'unit': unit, 'arm': arm, 'draw_store': draw, 'members': sorted(shown), 'format': 'v13s', 'system': SYSTEM_S, 'user': user,\n131:                'schema': rv.v13s_schema(sorted(shown), evidence_ids, theme_ids), 'family': family, 'passages': passages(draw, evidence_ids),\n141:    small = fmt == 'v13s'\n321:            family = joint['family'] if fmt == 'v13s' else pair_family(draw_store, total, dc)\n530:def packet_units(results, items):\n570:def packet_body(items, unit, arm, decision, per_card):\n652:def packets(out, key_dir):\n795:    elif cmd == 'plan-v13s' and len(sys.argv) == 3:                     # D737: the small format's plan, $0\n796:        plan(sys.argv[2], 'v13s')\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n   440\t  - no five-company or 2027 figure was taken as the answer;\n   441\t  - c8 proposed no NVIDIA-figure question.\n   442\t- **Missed:**\n   443\t  - P2's proxy clause: total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy, which old also misses;\n   444\t  - V: the rebuilt control's v47 was unsupported, and a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n   445\t- **Not shown:** that the merge answers better. The old answers were already supported, and the capex band was set by 09-06 history.\n   446\t- **Next:** superseded by D681, below.\n   447\t\n   448\t**The one next task: Alex chooses whether to authorize a targeted $0 test (D745).** The test covers:\n   449\t\n   450\t- the qualifier list adds \"estimated\", and readers see the allowed values;\n   451\t- one clarification: a band-deciding deduction or cut must show evidence for its size or a supported bound, else unresolved;\n   452\t- the test runs on S and on one case the passages do support;\n   453\t- before it, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n   454\t\n   455\tThe second re-read (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5):\n   456\t\n   457\t- **The readers are calibrated:** 5 of 5 controls pass.\n   458\t- **It stopped on two lines:**\n   459\t  - the qualifier-list gap;\n   460\t  - S has zero usable replies, because neither writer supports the data-center share that decides its band.\n   461\t- **E's Codex reply** shows the same weakness (a 15% cut).\n   462\t\n   463\tThe record of the D744 step:\n   464\t\n   465\t**Earlier next task (done, D745): Alex chooses whether to authorize one more bounded blinded re-read (D744).**\n   466\t\n   467\t- **Why it is needed.** The D743 re-read fixed the range rule, but stopped as written on Q10: both readers marked the permitted \"plus or\n   468\t  minus 2%\" midpoint, and both adjudicators rejected the mark.\n   469\t- **Council 58** recommends readers get the same rule text as adjudicators (system text, schema descriptions, definitions; one shared\n   470\t  source), plus five clean controls (midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported decisive assumption).\n10859:## D721 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 43/43b: the double-count premise is partly falsified by the frozen gold; recommend closing the design with nothing to build; D711's H4 reading corrected\n   194\t## 6. Writing prompts\n   195\t\n   196\t- **The prompt carries the standard, not new rules.** Every sentence about what to keep, reject, weigh or open traces to a line of the\n   197\t  step's standard (§7.1) or a decision; a sentence with no such line is a proposal for Alex, not an edit. A reject sentence must not\n   198\t  remove what the standard keeps: a missing amount, date or scale is never on its own a reason to drop a statement an analyst would note;\n   199\t  its weight is judged apart and may be lower (D240, Alex: \"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\").\n   200\t- **The prompt audit runs after every measurement and before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`). The whole\n   201\t  request (system, every category block, the fixed user sentences, every schema description and example) is read against the standard\n   202\t  clause by clause, in both directions, with a blind read-only second reader: each clause is consistent, contradicts, keeps wider, keeps\n   203\t  narrower or has no source. The procedure is audited too: a rule that decides by another test than the standard's, or an exception\n   204\t  written wider than the standard scopes it, contradicts the standard even when every sentence quotes it. Each failure class of the last\n   205\t  measurement gets one row (the request sentence, the standard's line, a verdict: contradiction, missing, abstract, misread, not the\n   206\t  prompt) in the arm's design document before a fix is written; a fix that answers a bench miss rather than a sentence is not written.\n   207\t  Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你需要同时仔细检查prompt和判法准则矛盾的问题，出现很多次了，你应该要做很细的检查\"; 2026-11-02: \"这个就是prompt的问题，你有没有深度的看promt 然后映射问题找矛盾\n   208\t  … 你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle 也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund\n   209\t  analyst skill, 不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill 以后固定跑，不然你每次都没有检讨到\".\n   210\t- **A test the model must apply to each statement is a check at the very end of the same prompt.** In one pass the model keeps what\n   211\t  looks like evidence and cites a rule afterwards; asked afterwards it judges correctly. So a closing check reads the list back and\n   212\t  returns only what passes, in the same call: the standard's own test, then its not-kept rows, each with its own boundary and the\n   213\t  standard's own examples, audited like any prompt text; a lost entry is judged by a blind reader. Alex, 2026-10-30: \"这个完全就是再prompt 的最后面要求做完一遍基于'你刚才说的复核条件'再跑一次；写在同一个提示词里面\".\n   214\t- **A prompt serves every company, never the bench.** Every analyst prompt is a general hedge-fund brain: each rule and example reads the\n   215\t  same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker. Examples teach from varied domains; none names the covered company, its\n   216\t  products or its numbers, and none is taken from a test article. A fix is written from the standard and from the request read as the\n   217\t  model reads it, not from the bench's misses, and a prompt is scrubbed for one-company or one-industry wording before any measurement.\n   218\t  Alex, 2026-10-29 (D237): \"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain. I keep on telling you this, and you keep on cherry\n   219\t  picking the results just to meet the metrics or the benchmarks\".\n   220\t- **Never grow a prompt to fix quality.** Read the whole request (system, schema, payload) as the model reads it, and list every\n   221\t  contradiction, rule written for another case, instruction that forces the defect, reference the model cannot read, payload field the\n   222\t  answer does not need, and call doing several jobs. Cut, merge or split. Add an instruction only when the audit shows one is missing,\n   223\t  and remove at least as much elsewhere. Report characters and prompt tokens before and after; growth needs a written reason.\n   224\t- **Trace a miss to the raw answer first.** Class it as not written, refused (by which check) or split. Check that the request shows what\n   225\t  the answer needs and the schema can express it before changing any wording.\n   226\t- **An example is an instruction.** It must agree with the standard, the schema and the code that reads the answer, or it goes.\n   227\t- **A classification the model must make is written as a closed set with examples:** two or three examples per class, the procedure\n   228\t  (walk the whole page, first paragraph to last) and the stopping point (\"a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence\n   229\t  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n   230\t  they replace is cut.\n   231\t- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n   232\t  for what a check refuses, and a retry that repairs a refusal is a symptom, not handling. Read every contract line against each gold\n   233\t  case it must accept and reject. Rebuild a stored request from the stored request, never from a ledger that moves, and check every\n   234\t  identity before a paid send.\n   235\t- **Build where code decides.** A decision code can make is moved out of the model, not sampled (identity only, §2).\n   236\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10859,10888p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | nl -ba -v 10859; rg -n 'q19|discount' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r43* docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REQUEST-AUDIT.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10859,10888p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | nl -ba -v 10859; rg -n 'q19|discount' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r43* docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REQUEST-AUDIT.md\"","aggregated_output":" 10859\t## D721 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 43/43b: the double-count premise is partly falsified by the frozen gold; recommend closing the design with nothing to build; D711's H4 reading corrected\n 10860\t\n 10861\t- **The rounds** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/`):\n 10862\t  - **Round 43** (`round43-brief.md`, `codex-r43.md`, `claude-r43.md`): DIAGNOSIS NEEDS CHANGES. old-1 has 26 explicit additions (not\n 10863\t    32). Six of Claude's became unclear (`1569`, `1691`, `1782`, `2313`, `2907`, `3293`). Codex also found overlapping quarterly\n 10864\t    adjustments, and argued that D263 supports keeping the target discount.\n 10865\t  - **Round 43b** (`round43b-brief.md`, `codex-r43b.md`, `claude-r43b.md`): PARTLY.\n 10866\t- **The check that changed the frame:** the frozen judgement gold (D262).\n 10867\t  - **q01**, total revenue at 07-14, band $400–435B: the gold builds a second half above consensus. It lists management's \"$20bn of\n 10868\t    Vera CPU sales by fiscal year-end\" among the supporting inputs, though not among the decisive items, and allocates it no dollar\n 10869\t    amount.\n 10870\t  - **q19**, stand-alone Vera: $8–18B.\n 10871\t  - **What follows:** the diagnosis's premise (\"a company-wide basis formed after the target was published already contains it\") is\n 10872\t    Claude's own view, not the ruler's. A judge sentence enforcing it, or the opposite, would have no source in the frozen standard\n 10873\t    (AGENTS §6, §7.1).\n 10874\t- **What survives, recorded as findings:**\n 10875\t  - **old-1 `4163`:** about the full $20B is added without volume shown, taking $414B to $434B across $431.2B. That is unsupported by\n 10876\t    the gold's discounting (q19).\n 10877\t  - **old-1 `978`:** treats a repeat as new, while acknowledging \"possible consensus inclusion\".\n 10878\t  - **The judge flips:** linked-1 `8752`, `8756` and `8770` change what the same $387B basis includes, with no explanation.\n 10879\t  - **v9's unsupported \"outside Data Center\" claim** (`8758`, then `8761`): the estimate moves about $20B, the rung does not.\n 10880\t  - **Overlapping quarterly adjustments in old-3:** 4 clear (`4298`, `4510`, `5195`, `5369`) and 6 related. No rung change is\n 10881\t    attributable to the overlap alone.\n 10882\t- **D711's exploratory H4, corrected:**\n 10883\t  - linked-1 made \"four unsupported increments, overlap unresolved\", not four proved double counts.\n 10884\t  - Against the paired control, only 07-15 differs in rung. The control reached about $417B without any CPU addition on 07-16..07-18\n 10885\t    (`8751`, `8755`, `8757`).\n 10886\t  - The pilot's registered verdict (inconclusive) is unchanged.\n 10887\t- **Consensus (Claude and Codex):** close the double-count design with nothing built or sent. The findings above go to Alex as a\n 10888\t  short list, not as a work order (AGENTS, Authority and scope).\nzsh:1: no matches found: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r43*\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; rg -n 'def _structure|does not rest|not used|why|def item|\"'^def passages'\"' \\\"\"'$d/calc_small.py\" \"$d/paid_gate.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,34p' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,35p' \\\"\"'$d/calc_small.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,35p' \\\"\"'$d/render_v13.py\"; rg -n '\"'def check_schema' src/hivemind/model.py; wc -l .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; rg -n 'def _structure|does not rest|not used|why|def item|\"'^def passages'\"' \\\"\"'$d/calc_small.py\" \"$d/paid_gate.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,34p' \\\"\"'$d/paid_gate.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,35p' \\\"\"'$d/calc_small.py\"; sed -n '\"'1,35p' \\\"\"'$d/render_v13.py\"; rg -n '\"'def check_schema' src/hivemind/model.py; wc -l .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:58:def passages(draw, evidence_ids):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:126:def item(unit, arm, draw, calls, family, shown, fmt='v13'):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:180:        if _text(a.get('why')):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:181:            a2['why'] = a['why']\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:187:            if outs or refs or reason or a.get('rung') is not None or not _text(a.get('why')):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:188:                errors.append(('S7', f'{v}: an unresolved answer has why, rung and reference_unavailable null, and no figure marked for it'))\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:371:def _structure(family, passages, calc, figs):\n\"\"\"The paid gate of judging-v13 (D725's registered test; D729, D730). Harness-only; nothing here is product code.\n\n  python3 paid_gate.py plan <out.json>                        # $0: build every request from the stored runs and check them\n  python3 paid_gate.py send <new out folder> <ceiling>        # paid: only at the ceiling Alex names (APPROVED_CEILING)\n  python3 paid_gate.py packets <out folder> <key folder>      # $0: blind reading packets, the arm hidden; the key kept outside the repo\n  python3 paid_gate.py verdict <out folder> <readings.json> <key folder>   # $0: the registered pass and stop lines\n\nThe test (D725, RESULT.md): the 09-20 total and Data Center pair in each of three stored draws, judged jointly (one v13 family call)\nand separately (each member alone, the same union of evidence, the same v13 format), three samples each; plus three samples each of\nthe O, S and E singletons. 9 + 18 + 9 = 36 first attempts. A calculation the checks refuse is retried once with its errors; a second\nrefusal makes the unit unresolved. The separate arm's two answers are then reconciled by the same checks over the pair's family.\n\"\"\"\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport random\nimport re\nimport secrets\nimport sqlite3\nimport sys\nimport threading\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[2]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\nsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\nimport calc_check as cc                                                 # noqa: E402\nimport calc_small as cs                                                 # noqa: E402\nimport render_v13 as rv                                                 # noqa: E402\nfrom hivemind import config, model                                      # noqa: E402\n\nRUN = REPO / 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run'\nCONFIG = REPO / 'poc/config/poc-v118.json'                              # its re-judging model setting: gpt-6-luna, medium, 16000 output tokens\nSYSTEM = (HERE / 'judging-v13-draft.txt').read_text()\n\"\"\"judging-v13s checks: the small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §2 [52] and [54], D737, D738). Harness-only. Code refuses an answer only for\nthe five named defects (S1 a number misread, S2 wrong arithmetic, S3/S8 a part above its whole or added into it, S4 two forecasts of one\nquarter, S5 an own estimate passed off as the market's) and for broken structure (S6, S7), and only where the calculation's own\nstructure decides it (D738). Everything else is recorded for readers.\n\nfrom_answer(data, family) -> the internal shape the harness reads ({'figures', 'answers', 'decode_errors'})\ncheck(family, passages, calc) -> {'errors': [(rule, detail)], 'recorded': [...], 'published': {view_id: [low, high, None]}}\n\"\"\"\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport re\n\nKINDS = {'source', 'computed', 'estimate'}\nBASES = {'reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'}\nEXTERNAL = {'consensus', 'guidance', 'third_party'}\nUNITS = {'USD bn', '%', 'x'}\nARITY = {'sum': (2, None), 'product': (2, None), 'difference': (2, 2), 'ratio': (2, 2), 'share': (2, 2), 'growth': (2, 2),\n         'apply_growth': (2, 2)}\nQUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\n                            'roughly', 'nearly', 'almost', 'below', 'under', 'near', 'estimated'], key=len, reverse=True)\nQUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\nQUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\nSCALE = {'trillion': 1000.0, 'tn': 1000.0, 't': 1000.0, 'billion': 1.0, 'bn': 1.0, 'b': 1.0, 'million': 0.001, 'mn': 0.001, 'm': 0.001}\nLIMIT = 1e9                                              # [54] a number beyond this is refused, not computed\nMAX_RANGE_LEAVES = 12\n\n_NUM = r'(\\d[\\d,]*(?:\\.\\d+)?)'\n_CUR = r'(?:US)?\\$\\s?'\n_SC = r'(?:\\s*(trillion|billion|million|bn|tn|mn|t|b|m)\\b)?'\n_MINUS = r'([-−–]\\s?)?'\n_PCT = r'\\s*(?:%|percent)'\n_BODIES = [   # [54] the complete numeric expressions, longest forms first; a quote_number must be one of them, whole\n    ('relative', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*,?\\s*\\(?\\s*(?:plus or minus|\\+/-|±)\\s*' + _NUM + r'\\s*%\\s*\\)?'),\n    ('range', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*(?:–|—|-|to)\\s*(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n    ('range', r'between\\s+' + _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s+and\\s+(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n\"\"\"Render one stored family's judging-v13 request (old-2 calls 8729 and 8737 on 2026-09-20) and check a conforming answer through the\nv13 schema (model.check_schema) and calc_check. Harness-only, $0, no model call. Usage: python3 render_v13.py [out-prefix]\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport sqlite3\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[2]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\nsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\nimport calc_check as cc  # noqa: E402\nimport calc_small  # noqa: E402\nimport gate  # noqa: E402\nfrom hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\n\nS, N = {'type': 'string'}, {'type': 'number'}\n\n\ndef nullable(schema):\n    return {'anyOf': [schema, {'type': 'null'}]}\n\n\ndef obj(props, desc=None):\n    out = {'type': 'object', 'properties': props, 'required': list(props), 'additionalProperties': False}\n    if desc:\n        out['description'] = desc\n    return out\n\n\ndef v13_schema(member_ids, evidence_ids, theme_ids):\n    what = obj({k: S for k in ('population', 'measure', 'scope', 'period')},\n               'What the number measures: population, measure (the quantity, such as revenue or units; never its basis), scope, period.')\n    figure = obj({\n        'id': S, 'what': what,\n184:def check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n      86 .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md | sed -n '1,100p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '371,416p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py | sed -n '530,575p;684,755p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md | sed -n '1,100p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '371,416p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py | sed -n '530,575p;684,755p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tname: prompt-audit\n     3\tdescription: After every measurement of an extraction configuration, and before any prompt change, map each failure class in the stored draws to the sentence of the request that produced it, check that sentence against the frozen standard and the prompt principles, and write the audit table. Alex, 2026-11-02 - \"你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund analyst skill，不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill以后固定跑\".\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Prompt audit: failures → the request's sentences → the standard → the principle\n     7\t\n     8\tRun this after every measured configuration (three draws scored on the frozen ruler) and before any edit to a prompt file, a schema\n     9\tdescription or a takes line. A measurement without this audit is a score without a diagnosis (AGENTS §7.2, §6). The audit is read,\n    10\tnot scripted: the scripts below only produce the lists to read.\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Inputs\n    13\t\n    14\t- The arm's run folders (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/arms/<arm>/d1..3`) and the baseline's.\n    15\t- The frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1): the extraction standard `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (version 3, D243, with D250's\n    16\t  boundaries), golden-v5, the questions standard, the judgement gold. Never edited; every disagreement is a gap in the system.\n    17\t- The prompt principles (AGENTS §6 and §8): the prompt carries the standard and no new rule; it teaches a general hedge-fund\n    18\t  analyst, so every sentence and example reads the same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker, names no covered\n    19\t  company, product or number, and takes no example from a test article; a classification is a closed set with examples; never grow a\n    20\t  prompt to fix quality without cutting as much elsewhere; a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field; code owns\n    21\t  identity, never importance.\n    22\t\n    23\t## 1. Dump the request as the model reads it\n    24\t\n    25\tOne stored request of the configuration (any article), printed whole: the system text (the role), every user block before the item\n    26\t(category block, subjects, research reference, open questions with their takes lines, research groups), the closing sentence, the\n    27\tend-of-request check, and every schema field description (evidence and candidates). Read it top to bottom once. List every\n    28\tinstruction that decides keep, reject, link, open, or a field value, with its location (role paragraph, check row, schema field,\n    29\ttakes line, category block). This list is the thing the failures are mapped onto.\n    30\t\n    31\t```sh\n    32\tpython3 - <<'PY'\n    33\timport sqlite3, json\n    34\ta = sqlite3.connect('file:<run folder>/archive.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    35\treq = json.loads(a.execute(\"select request_json from model_calls where purpose='extract' and request_json like '%<title fragment>%' order by call_id desc limit 1\").fetchone()[0])\n    36\tprint(req['messages'][0]['content']); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].split('[Item]')[0]); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].rsplit('\\n\\n', 2)[-2:])\n    37\tprint(json.dumps({k: v.get('description') for k, v in req['response_format']['json_schema']['schema']['properties']['evidence']['items']['properties'].items() if v.get('description')}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))\n    38\tPY\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\t## 2. Produce the failure lists (all at no model cost, on the stored draws)\n    42\t\n    43\t| List | Script | What it gives |\n    44\t|---|---|---|\n    45\t| Matter states across draws | `harness/matter_states.py arm=d1,d2,d3 base=d1,d2,d3` | every gold core/supporting matter: extracted or reaching in 3/2/1/0 draws; the systematic misses (0 of 3) and the coin flips (1 of 3) |\n    46\t| Written noise by the standard's row | the `rows_all` count in `design-v72.md` §10 (the gold's `not_kept[].row`) | which row of the standard the written noise falls under, with examples |\n    47\t| No-home drops | `harness/dropped_census.py d1 d2 d3` | gold matters written but dropped, with the questions open at the time |\n    48\t| Where links go | `harness/links_by_question.py d1 d2 d3 --sample 20` | the pile per question, the revenue-sink share, statements under several questions, noise under revenue |\n    49\t| Quote refusals | the `dropped` reasons of the handovers (`quote not in item`), each diffed against its closest source window | paraphrase against a word or two off |\n    50\t| Question recall | `harness/question_recall.py d1 d2 d3` | must_open / may_open covered by seeds and by article-opened questions |\n    51\t| Fields | 15 random stored statements with speaker, original_who, original_day, tier | read against the quote and the item's day and publisher |\n    52\t\n    53\tFor every list, read the items, not the counts: the raw answer beside the article for every systematic miss (a miss the scorer reports\n    54\tmay be the matter written in other words: check before counting it), a sample of twenty for every pile.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 3. Map each failure class to the request\n    57\t\n    58\tFor each class with more than a handful of cases, fill one row:\n    59\t\n    60\t| Failure class (count, share) | The sentence(s) of the request that produced it, quoted | The standard's line on the same case (section, decision) | Verdict | The model's own account (when the sentence is not obvious) |\n    61\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    62\t\n    63\tVerdicts, one of: **contradiction** (two sentences of the request pull opposite ways, or a request sentence removes what the standard\n    64\tkeeps, D240), **missing** (no sentence decides the case; the model guesses, a coin flip per send), **abstract** (a clause without an\n    65\texample, read differently each send), **misread** (the sentence is right, the model does not apply it; needs the model's own account\n    66\tbefore any change), **not the prompt** (code, scorer or sampler).\n    67\t\n    68\tThe model's own account: continue the stored conversation with one question about one case (`harness/probe_v72_link.py`,\n    69\t`probe_v72_missing.py`), a cent a case, for every \"misread\" and for any class whose sentence is not obvious from reading.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 4. Check every proposed fix against the principles before writing it\n    72\t\n    73\t- It is written from the standard's line, not from the failing article: the example comes from another domain, names no covered\n    74\t  company, product or number, and reads the same for every industry. A fix that only recovers the bench's misses is benchmark\n    75\t  farming and is not written.\n    76\t- It removes a contradiction, supplies a missing definition at its owner (schema field, takes line, check row, category block), or\n    77\t  replaces an abstract clause with a closed set and examples. It never adds a rule the standard does not hold.\n    78\t- It cuts at least as much as it adds, or the growth has a written reason; report characters before and after.\n    79\t- One class per change; the change is proved on the stored requests of that class, three sends as stored against three changed,\n    80\t  with the noise guards of the same articles; the whole bench accepts it (three draws) only when Alex asks for a run.\n    81\t\n    82\t## 5. Output\n    83\t\n    84\tWrite the table and the verdicts into the arm's design document (a numbered section), one row per class, in the order of the loss;\n    85\tthe report to Alex states each class in a reader's words (what the system does wrong, which sentence makes it do that, what the\n    86\tstandard says), never in the loop's own words (AGENTS, \"Authority and scope\": explain as to a sixteen-year-old).\n   371\tdef _structure(family, passages, calc, figs):\n   372\t    errors = list(calc.get('decode_errors') or [])\n   373\t    ids = [f.get('id') for f in calc['figures']]\n   374\t    for fid in sorted({i for i in ids if ids.count(i) > 1}, key=str):\n   375\t        errors.append(('S7', f'{fid} is defined twice'))\n   376\t    for f in calc['figures']:\n   377\t        fid = f.get('id')\n   378\t        if not isinstance(fid, str) or not fid:\n   379\t            errors.append(('S7', 'a figure has no id'))\n   380\t            continue\n   381\t        if fid in passages:\n   382\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure id must not be a passage id'))\n   383\t        if f.get('kind') not in KINDS or f.get('basis') not in BASES or f.get('unit') not in UNITS:\n   384\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: kind, basis or unit outside the closed set'))\n   385\t            continue\n   386\t        numbers = [f.get(k) for k in ('value', 'low', 'high') if isinstance(f.get(k), (int, float))]\n   387\t        if any(not math.isfinite(n) or abs(n) > LIMIT for n in numbers):\n   388\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a number that is not finite or beyond {LIMIT:g}'))\n   389\t            continue\n   390\t        has_value = isinstance(f.get('value'), (int, float))\n   391\t        has_low, has_high = isinstance(f.get('low'), (int, float)), isinstance(f.get('high'), (int, float))\n   392\t        if has_value == (has_low or has_high) or has_low != has_high:\n   393\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure has value, or low and high, never both and never one end alone'))\n   394\t        elif has_low and has_high and f['low'] > f['high']:\n   395\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: low is above high'))\n   396\t        kind = f['kind']\n   397\t        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\n   398\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n   399\t        if kind != 'computed' and (f.get('inputs') or f.get('operation')):\n   400\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs and operation are for computed figures only'))\n   401\t        if kind != 'estimate' and f.get('rests_on'):\n   402\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n   403\t        if kind == 'source':\n   404\t            if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   405\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source cannot have basis forecast'))\n   406\t            if not f.get('evidence') or not f.get('quote_number'):\n   407\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source needs evidence_id and quote_number'))\n   408\t        elif kind == 'computed':\n   409\t            ins, op = f.get('inputs') or [], f.get('operation')\n   410\t            if op not in ARITY or not ins:\n   411\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a computed figure needs inputs and an operation from the closed set'))\n   412\t            else:\n   413\t                lo_n, hi_n = ARITY[op]\n   414\t                if len(ins) < lo_n or (hi_n is not None and len(ins) > hi_n):\n   415\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: {op} takes ' + (f'exactly {lo_n}' if hi_n == lo_n else f'{lo_n} or more') + f' inputs, not {len(ins)}'))\n   416\t            for i in ins:\n   530\tdef packet_units(results, items):\n   531\t    \"\"\"Every packet the run gives, as (unit, arm, draw) -> (decision, per-card source): the joint and singleton answers from the finals,\n   532\t    the separate arm's reconciled pairs with each card's own separate answer.\"\"\"\n   533\t    units = {}\n   534\t    for key, entry in results['finals'].items():\n   535\t        unit, arm, draw = key.split('|')\n   536\t        if not arm.startswith('separate-'):\n   537\t            first = first_attempt(entry)                    # [47e.1] the first attempt is scored on both arms\n   538\t            units[(unit, arm, draw)] = (first.get('decision'), {v: first for v in items[(unit, arm)]['members']})\n   539\t    for key, entry in results['reconciled'].items():\n   540\t        unit, draw = key.split('|')\n   541\t        per_card = {a.split('-', 1)[1]: first_attempt(results['finals'].get(f'{unit}|{a}|{draw}', {})) for (u, a) in items\n   542\t                    if u == unit and a.startswith('separate-')}\n   543\t        units[(unit, 'separate', draw)] = (entry.get('decision'), per_card)\n   544\t    return units\n   545\t\n   546\t\n   547\tdef card_lines(base, fam, v, token, data, final_label, prefix=''):\n   548\t    \"\"\"One card's answer as a reader sees it: the passages it names, its own figures, its recomputed outcome and assumptions, the card.\n   549\t    Returns (lines, ids a ground may name).\"\"\"\n   550\t    if not data:\n   551\t        return [f'  {final_label}'], set()\n   552\t    fmt = base.get('format')\n   553\t    calc = decode(data, fmt, fam)\n   554\t    try:\n   555\t        checked = checker(fmt).check(fam, base['passages'], calc)\n   556\t    except Exception as error:\n   557\t        checked = {'errors': [('checker', str(error))], 'recorded': [], 'published': {}}\n   558\t    try:\n   559\t        norm, recorded, named = card_view(calc, v, checked.get('recorded', []), prefix, checker(fmt))\n   560\t    except StopIteration:\n   561\t        return ['  The answer has no card for this question.'], set()\n   562\t    lines = ['  Passages, as the request showed them:\\n' + '\\n'.join('  ' + base['shown'].get(e, f'- {e} (not shown)') for e in named),\n   563\t             '  Figures (declared):\\n' + '\\n'.join(f'  - {json.dumps(f, ensure_ascii=False)}' for f in norm['figures']),\n   564\t             '  Recomputed outcome: ' + json.dumps((checked.get('published') or {}).get(v)),\n   565\t             '  Recorded assumptions: ' + json.dumps(recorded),\n   566\t             f'  Card {token}: ' + json.dumps(norm['answers'][0], ensure_ascii=False)]\n   567\t    return lines, set(named) | {f['id'] for f in norm['figures']}\n   568\t\n   569\t\n   570\tdef packet_body(items, unit, arm, decision, per_card):\n   571\t    \"\"\"[47d.3, 47e.1] One scoring packet: each card's scored (first) answer only. No attempt history, no code decision and no check code,\n   572\t    which would tell separate calls from one joint call; the verdict applies the code's decision itself. Returns (lines, grounds, members).\"\"\"\n   573\t    base = items[(unit, 'joint' if unit.startswith('pair') else 'single')]\n   574\t    grounds, lines = set(), []\n   575\t    for number, v in enumerate(base['members'], 1):\n   684\t\n   685\t\n   686\tdef final_readings(pid, cards, members, grounds=()):\n   687\t    \"\"\"[47.4] Two independent readings per card (A, B), each question answered yes/no with its ground; a third decides each\n   688\t    disagreement. Returns ({card: {question: answer}}, problems).\"\"\"\n   689\t    problems, final = [], {}\n   690\t    if sorted(cards) != sorted(members):\n   691\t        return None, [f'{pid}: cards {sorted(cards)} are not the unit\\'s {sorted(members)}']\n   692\t    for v, r in cards.items():\n   693\t        final[v] = {}\n   694\t        for q in QUESTIONS:\n   695\t            a, b = (r.get(x, {}).get(q, {}) for x in ('A', 'B'))\n   696\t            for name, x in (('A', a), ('B', b)):\n   697\t                if x.get('answer') not in ('yes', 'no') or not _grounded(x.get('ground'), grounds):\n   698\t                    problems.append(f'{pid} {v} {q}: reader {name} gave no yes/no with a ground from this packet')\n   699\t            if a.get('answer') == b.get('answer'):\n   700\t                final[v][q] = a.get('answer')\n   701\t            else:\n   702\t                t = r.get('third', {}).get(q, {})\n   703\t                if t.get('answer') not in ('yes', 'no') or not _grounded(t.get('ground'), grounds):\n   704\t                    problems.append(f'{pid} {v} {q}: the readers disagree and no third reading decides')\n   705\t                final[v][q] = t.get('answer')\n   706\t    return final, problems\n   707\t\n   708\t\n   709\tdef verdict(out, readings_path, key_dir):\n   710\t    \"\"\"[47e.4] Seal the readings into the run (readings.json, created once), compute D725's lines once and write verdict.json bound to\n   711\t    the readings, the key and the packets. A second verdict on the run is refused; `replay` recomputes and compares.\"\"\"\n   712\t    if (out / 'verdict.json').exists() or (out / 'readings.json').exists():\n   713\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} already has sealed readings or a verdict; refused (use replay)')\n   714\t    data = Path(readings_path).read_bytes()\n   715\t    with open(out / 'readings.json', 'xb') as handle:\n   716\t        handle.write(data)\n   717\t    summary = score(out, key_dir)\n   718\t    frozen = json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())\n   719\t    record = {'readings_sha256': hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest(), 'key_commitment': key_commitment(frozen),\n   720\t              'packets_sha256': hashlib.sha256((out / 'packets.md').read_bytes()).hexdigest() if (out / 'packets.md').exists() else None,\n   721\t              'run_digest': run_digest(out), 'summary': summary}\n   722\t    with open(out / 'verdict.json', 'x') as handle:\n   723\t        json.dump(record, handle, indent=1)\n   724\t    return summary\n   725\t\n   726\t\n   727\tdef replay(out, key_dir):\n   728\t    \"\"\"Recompute the verdict from the sealed readings and compare it with verdict.json.\"\"\"\n   729\t    record = json.loads((out / 'verdict.json').read_text())\n   730\t    data = (out / 'readings.json').read_bytes()\n   731\t    same = (hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() == record['readings_sha256'] and record['run_digest'] == run_digest(out)\n   732\t            and record['key_commitment'] == key_commitment(json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())))\n   733\t    summary = score(out, key_dir)\n   734\t    matches = same and summary == record['summary']\n   735\t    print(json.dumps({'reproduced': matches}, indent=1))\n   736\t    return matches\n   737\t\n   738\t\n   739\tdef score(out, key_dir):\n   740\t    \"\"\"D725's registered lines on the sealed readings, bound to this run.\"\"\"\n   741\t    frozen = json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())\n   742\t    submitted = json.loads((out / 'readings.json').read_text())\n   743\t    problems = [] if frozen.get('run_digest') == run_digest(out) else ['the packet key does not belong to this run']   # [47b.4]\n   744\t    text, key, units = render_packets(out, frozen)                  # [47c.2] every packet rendered again from the run\n   745\t    if sorted(tuple(u) for _, u in frozen['order']) != sorted(units):\n   746\t        problems.append('the key does not map one packet to each unit of the run')\n   747\t    sha = hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()\n   748\t    packets_path = out / 'packets.md'\n   749\t    written = packets_path.read_text() if packets_path.exists() else ''\n   750\t    if not written.startswith(f'Key commitment: {key_commitment(frozen)}\\n'):      # [47d.1] the mapping the readers' text committed to\n   751\t        problems.append('the key is not the one packets.md committed to')\n   752\t    if hashlib.sha256(written.encode()).hexdigest() != sha:\n   753\t        problems.append('packets.md is not the text the run and the key give')\n   754\t    if submitted.get('packets_sha256') != sha:\n   755\t        problems.append('the readings were not made on the frozen packets')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport json, sys\nr = Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers'\\\\'')\nsys.path[:0] = [str(r.resolve()), str(Path('\\\\''src'\\\\'').resolve())]\nimport calc_small as cs\nfrom hivemind import model\nroot = Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\ndef load(file):\n    return json.loads((root / file).read_text())\ndef unresolved(case, why, cites):\n    a = load(case + '\\\\''/answers/claude.json'\\\\'')\n    a['\\\\''figures'\\\\''] = []\n    a['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0].update(shape='\\\\''unresolved'\\\\'', rung=None, reference_unavailable=None, reasons='\\\\'''\\\\'', why=why, cites=cites, pushes=[], theme_pushes=[])\n    return a\ndef check(label, case, a):\n    it = load(case + '\\\\''/item.json'\\\\'')\n    schema_errors = model.check_schema(a, it['\\\\''schema'\\\\''])\n    calc = cs.from_answer(a, it['\\\\''family'\\\\''])\n    result = cs.check(it['\\\\''family'\\\\''], it['\\\\''passages'\\\\''], calc)\n    print(label, '\\\\''schema:'\\\\'', schema_errors, '\\\\''checks:'\\\\'', result['\\\\''errors'\\\\''], '\\\\''decision:'\\\\'', cs.publish_decision(it['\\\\''family'\\\\''], calc, result), '\\\\''numeric outcomes:'\\\\'', result['\\\\''published'\\\\''])\ncheck('\\\\''K-unsupported'\\\\'', '\\\\''E'\\\\'', load('\\\\''reread2-controls/K-unsupported.json'\\\\''))\ncheck('\\\\''Stored direct cut'\\\\'', '\\\\''E'\\\\'', load('\\\\''E/answers/codex.json'\\\\''))\ncheck('\\\\''Stored P-v8'\\\\'', '\\\\''P-v8'\\\\'', load('\\\\''P-v8/answers/claude.json'\\\\''))\ncheck('\\\\''P-v8 unjustified unresolved'\\\\'', '\\\\''P-v8'\\\\'', unresolved('\\\\''P-v8'\\\\'', '\\\\''The evidence is insufficient to forecast.'\\\\'', []))\ns = unresolved('\\\\''S'\\\\'', '\\\\''e5014 sizes total capex for the four, e1947 sizes AI infrastructure, e4766 sizes data-center capex for five, and e4082 does not identify the four buyers. None gives the four buyers’ data-center share or a bound that settles which side of \"'$700 billion it lies on.'\"'\\\\'', ['\\\\''e5014'\\\\'', '\\\\''e1947'\\\\'', '\\\\''e4766'\\\\'', '\\\\''e4082'\\\\''])\ncheck('\\\\''S justified unresolved'\\\\'', '\\\\''S'\\\\'', s)\ns['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''why'\\\\''] = '\\\\'''\\\\''\ncheck('\\\\''S unresolved missing reason'\\\\'', '\\\\''S'\\\\'', s)\nbase = 75.25 + 89\ncompany_h2 = 113.4 * (1 + 1.12)\nprint('\\\\''P-v8 H1:'\\\\'', base, '\\\\''H2 company:'\\\\'', company_h2, '\\\\''mix thresholds:'\\\\'', [(edge-base)/company_h2 for edge in (350,400)], '\\\\''FY segment at reported Q2 mix:'\\\\'', base+company_h2*.925)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
