\": null,\n   159\t      \"high\": null,\n   160\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   161\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   162\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   163\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   164\t      \"inputs\": [\"F5\", \"F6\"],\n   165\t      \"operation\": \"product\",\n   166\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   167\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   168\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"],\n   169\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   170\t    }\n   171\t  ],\n   172\t  \"answers\": [\n   173\t    {\n   174\t      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n   175\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   176\t      \"rung\": 3,\n   177\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   178\t      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluati\n   179\t      \"why\": null,\n   180\t      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5403\"],\n     1\t{\"figures\": [{\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"low\": null, \"high\n    10\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    11\tfrom calc_small import QUALIFIERS  # noqa: E402\n    12\t\n    13\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    14\tnot a defect):\n    15\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    16\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    17\t  format rules permit.\n    18\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    19\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    20\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    21\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    22\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    23\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    24\t  quarter.\n    25\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    26\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    27\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    28\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    29\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    30\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    31\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    32\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    33\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"agent_message","text":"**Run with the changes below.** At $745B of total spending, a 90% data-center share gives $671B and a 95% share gives $708B. The passages leave the share open, so choosing either percentage can decide the answer without evidence. [S passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:27) · [Council 59](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r59.md:7)\n\n1. **Serious — Say what must support the estimate, and qualify “discounted by default.”** The proposed sentence is general and captures when to leave an answer unresolved. Council 59 also requires the writer to **show** what supports the amount or the bound. “One estimate” can miss a calculation where several adjustments together decide the band. And the default discount gives a reason to expect a cut, while leaving its size open. E’s saved $34B answer shows what happens when the writer fills in that gap with a 15% cut. Keep the discount principle and make it subject to the closing evidence requirement. Replace the existing unresolved sentence with:\n\n   > If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap.\n\n   At the earlier instruction, change “is discounted by default” to “is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below.” Keep the condition for taking the target in full. This preserves the existing discount principle and makes clear what it can justify. [Current instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [E’s 85% realization estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:75) · [D745 and Alex’s answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11502)\n\n2. **Minor — P-v8 is a fair positive case.** The passages give $75.25B of actual Data Center revenue in Q1 and $89B in Q2, a reported segment share that rose to 92.5%, and $108B ±2% of Q3 company guidance with Data Center driving growth. Management expects hyperscaler growth to pick up in Q4 as supply builds. Morningstar also gives a direct $361B full-year segment estimate. As a check, applying the reported 92.5% mix to the saved Claude answer’s second-half company forecast gives about $387B for the year, inside the $350–400B band. Require a supported segment forecast without prescribing the saved figure or treating 92.5% as a proven future share. [Q1 actual](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt:579) · [Q2 actual and Morningstar estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt:588) · [Q3 guidance and growth driver](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt:458) · [Reported mix and Q4 outlook](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt:610) · [Saved calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/claude.json:447)\n\n3. **Serious — Test both reader errors.** The 140% E control is valid for spotting an unsupported forecast. The saved $34B E answer is a sharper replacement because its unsupported cut is the exact failure under review. Keep the P-v8 vague unresolved control and add a **justified unresolved S** control. Its reason should explain why the four companies’ total capex, their AI infrastructure spending, and five companies’ data-center capex cannot settle the four-company data-center band. Expect **no, no, yes**, respectively. I checked both E negatives and both unresolved forms in memory. All passed the schema and code checks, so the readers’ verdicts are what distinguish them. Update the first-reader instructions and adjudication together. For a forecast, ask what evidence supports the assumption that decides the band. For an unresolved answer, ask what gap remains after reading the strongest relevant passages. The current first-reader form asks only what puts a forecast in its band. [Existing E control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py:74) · [S spending passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:75) · [First-reader instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py:22) · [Adjudication instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:21) · [Unresolved answer check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:185)\n\n4. **Serious — Register exactly what earns the paid re-send request.** D745’s written test names S and one supported case. It also names E as the same failure. Resolve that difference explicitly when registering the proposed six-writer scope. With E included, require six unedited first replies, nine blinded packets including the three controls, and 18 first readings plus any adjudications. The existing inventory expects all six historical cases, and the old control builder requires joint P. Register the smaller inventory explicitly. Every S and E reply needs a settled **yes** on either a forecast or its reason for leaving the answer unresolved. Both P-v8 replies need a settled **yes** on a forecast. A remaining support split leaves the run inconclusive and sends it back to council. Keep the existing refusal and defect stops. A clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request re-send, which is where the production model is measured. [D745 scope](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11494) · [Existing inventory](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:118) · [Existing control builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py:54) · [Registered stops and paid sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n\n**Verdict: run with listed changes.** This review was read-only."}}
